## Best Practices

### 1. Use DataFrames/SQL Instead of RDD

```python
# Avoid (slower)
rdd = sc.textFile("data.txt")
result = rdd.map(lambda x: x.split(",")).map(lambda x: (x[0], int(x[1]))).reduceByKey(lambda a, b: a + b)

# Prefer (faster)
df = spark.read.csv("data.csv", header=True, inferSchema=True)
result = df.groupBy("name").sum("value")
```

### 2. Avoid Unnecessary Shuffles

```python
# Avoid
df1.join(df2).select(...).groupBy(...).count()  # Multiple shuffles

# Prefer
df = df1.join(df2).select(...).groupBy(...).count()  # One query
```

### 3. Use Broadcast for Small Data

```python
# Avoid (causes shuffle)
df.join(small_df, "key")

# Prefer (no shuffle)
small_df = spark.broadcast(small_df)
df.join(small_df, "key")
```

### 4. Cache When Reusing

```python
df = spark.read.csv("huge_file.csv")
df.cache()
result1 = df.filter(df.age > 30).count()
result2 = df.filter(df.salary > 50000).count()  # Reuses cache
df.unpersist()
```

### 5. Use Efficient File Formats

```python
# Avoid (slow, large)
df.write.csv("output.csv")

# Prefer (fast, compressed)
df.write.parquet("output.parquet")
```

### 6. Partition Data Wisely

```python
# Avoid (too many small files)
df.repartition(1).write.parquet("output")

# Prefer
df.repartition(50).write.parquet("output")

# Check partitions
df.rdd.getNumPartitions()
```

### 7. Write Idiomatic Code

```python
# Avoid (verbose)
from pyspark.sql.functions import col
df.select(col("name")).filter(col("age") > 30)

# Prefer
df.select("name").filter(df.age > 30)
```

