**Q17: Explain columnar storage (Parquet) vs row storage (CSV) and when to use each.**

A:

| Aspect | Parquet | CSV |
|--------|---------|-----|
| **Storage** | Columnar | Row-based |
| **Compression** | 10-100x | 2-5x |
| **Query Speed** | Very fast (column pruning) | Slow (must read all) |
| **Schema Evolution** | Supported | Not supported |
| **Size** | Small | Large |
| **Human Readable** | No | Yes |
| **Spark Use** | Optimized | Supported |

**Performance Comparison**:
```python
# CSV: Must scan entire file
df = spark.read.csv("data.csv")
result = df.select("name").filter(df.age > 30).count()
# Reads: ALL columns (wasteful)

# Parquet: Only reads needed columns
df = spark.read.parquet("data.parquet")
result = df.select("name").filter(df.age > 30).count()
# Reads: Only "name" and "age" columns (efficient)
```

**When to use**:
- **Parquet**: For production data lakes, long-term storage, analytical queries
- **CSV**: For data exchange, human-readable reports, one-time loads

**Interview Talking Point**: Always use Parquet for Spark. It's optimized, compresses well, and supports schema evolution.

