**Q18: How do you handle late-arriving data in Spark Streaming?**

A: Use watermarks to define when to close a window.

```python
spark = SparkSession.builder.getOrCreate()

df = spark.readStream \
    .format("socket") \
    .option("host", "localhost") \
    .option("port", 9999) \
    .load()

# Define watermark: consider data more than 10 minutes late as invalid
result = df.withWatermark("eventTime", "10 minutes") \
    .groupBy(
        window(col("eventTime"), "5 minutes"),
        col("event_type")
    ) \
    .count() \
    .writeStream \
    .outputMode("update") \
    .format("console") \
    .start()

result.awaitTermination()

# Output mode explains how results are updated:
# append - new rows only (fast, but cannot modify old windows)
# update - changed rows (slower, allows late data to update window)
# complete - all rows (slowest, but most accurate)
```

**Interview Talking Point**: Watermarks enable you to handle the trade-off between latency and accuracy. They're essential for production streaming systems.

